hen Opus 5.5 resigned as White. The opening was level, with Black's lead at -0.2 after turn 5. Then Opus 5.5 lost 16.6 points with D6 on turn 8 and kept giving points back, averaging 10.5 lost per move with 8 blunders. The 12k had 4 blunders of its own and a 5.1 average, but Opus 5.5's losses were larger and came more often.", "moments": [{"turn": 8, "text": "Opus 5.5's D6 stretched a White wall over Black's C5-D5-E5 line, but it lost 16.6 points and moved Black's lead from +0.2 to +16.9. The engine preferred B5. This was the first big step away from an even game."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "The 12k's A4 lost 14.6 points and cut Black's lead from +21.8 to +7.2. It was the best chance Opus 5.5 had to get back into the game."}, {"turn": 14, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with D3, which lost 17.6 points and pushed Black's lead from +11.1 to +28.7. The engine's choice was B6. The earlier gain was gone, and the next several moves never closed the gap again."}, {"turn": 22, "text": "B1 lost 19.5 points and moved the lead from +28.9 to +48.5. The engine wanted G7 in the open space on the right. Opus 5.5 kept playing in the bottom-left corner while Black still held the right side of the board."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "B2 was the worst move of the game, losing 39.9 points and taking Black's lead from +32.5 to +72.5. Again the engine preferred G7. After this the game was decided, and the 12k finished it with C7 on turn 29."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberty count with a danger flag, and count.py flood-fills empty regions to tally territory with optional dead stones removed; both only inspect the current position, with no candidate-move scoring or look-ahead.", "stop": false}}